                  <div style="text-align: center;"><br>
                    <strong>🌨️Winter Storm Severity Index</strong><br>
                    <a href="https://www.weather.gov/images/erh/gis/CAR_Ice.png"
                      title="Click for larger image"> </a></div>
                  <div style="text-align: center;"><a href="https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/wssi/images/WSSI_Overall_Day1_CAR_Day1.png"><img
                        src="https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/wssi/images/WSSI_Overall_Day1_CAR_Day1.png"
                        alt="Winter Storm Severity Index" class="weather-image"
                        style="max-width: 100%; width: 475px; border-radius: 1px; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px;"></a></div>
                </div>
                <div style="text-align: center;" class="small-note">
                  <p data-pm-slice="1 1 []"><em><strong><span>Note:</span></strong><span>
                        The Winter Storm Severity Index (WSSI) illustrates the
                        potential severity of winter weather impacts, not
                        snowfall amounts or the probability of a storm
                        occurring. This Day 1 outlook covers a 24-hour forecast
                        period; consult the chart for its valid times.<br>
                      </span></em></p>
                </div>
                <hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #eeeeee;
                  margin: 25px 0;">
                <!-- Narrative Forecasting -->
                <div style="text-align: center;"><strong style="font-size: 20px;"><span>The
                      Week Ahead for Fort Kent &amp; the Saint John Valley<br>
                    </span></strong><strong style="font-size: 18px;"><span><br>
                    </span></strong>
                  <div style="text-align: center; font-size: 15px;"><strong style="font-size: 18px;">November
                      26 – December 2, 2026</strong>
                    <div style="text-align: center;"><strong style="font-size: 18px;"></strong><br>
                      <span style="font-size: 12px;">Updated November 26 at 8:30
                        AM EST</span></div>
                  </div>
                  <div style="text-align: left; font-size: 15px;"><strong><span
                        style="font-size: 18px;"><span><br>
                        </span></span></strong></div>
                  <div style="text-align: left; font-size: 15px;"><strong><span
                        style="font-size: 18px;"><span></span></span></strong><strong>Thursday,
                      November 26 – Saturday, November 28</strong>
                    <p>🦃🍂 <b data-path-to-node="3,0" data-index-in-node="5">Happy
                        Thanksgiving from Apex Wx!</b> Wishing you a safe and
                      warm holiday! 🥧🍁</p>
                    <p><strong>Overview</strong></p>
                    <p><span><span>Following another frigid start to the day,
                          with temperatures reaching 10°F at the Apex Wx station
                          this morning, Canadian high pressure maintains quiet
                          Thanksgiving weather. An approaching low-pressure
                          system then draws warmer, moisture-rich air northward
                          over the colder air mass established earlier in the
                          week.</span>The resulting temperature contrast
                        supports <strong>freezing rain Friday morning</strong>,
                        with warmer air aloft overriding a shallow subfreezing
                        layer near the surface. Sheltered portions of the Saint
                        John and Fish River valleys may retain colder
                        temperatures longer, potentially extending freezing
                        precipitation in low-lying areas.</span></p>
                    <p><span><span>As surface temperatures rise, freezing rain
                          gradually transitions to ordinary rain. Colder air
                          returning Friday night supports a <strong>mixture of
                            rain and snow </strong>that lingers into Saturday.</span>
                        Although little or no ice accumulation is expected, even
                        brief freezing rain may create slippery untreated
                        surfaces. Lingering precipitation diminishes Saturday as
                        the system departs, although overnight cooling
                        introduces additional refreezing concerns. The
                        persistence of shallow cold air and timing of
                        precipitation transitions remain the primary
                        uncertainties for holiday travel.</span></p>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Thanksgiving Day:</strong></span></span><span>
                        </span><span>Sunny skies and relatively quiet conditions
                          prevail as Canadian high pressure maintains its
                          influence, providing favorable weather for daytime
                          holiday travel.</span><span> Afternoon temperatures
                          reach the upper 30s before falling toward 30°F later
                          in the day, with light south winds of 0–3 mph.
                          Increasing cloud cover develops Thursday night as
                          another low-pressure system approaches, with
                          temperatures falling into the lower 20s and east winds
                          around 2 mph. A 30% chance of snow develops after 5 AM
                          Friday. Little or no ice accumulation is expected
                          overnight, although the approaching system introduces
                          more substantial winter-weather concerns for Friday
                          morning.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Friday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Freezing rain develops as warmer air advances over the
                          colder surface layer, creating potentially <strong>slippery
                            conditions during the morning</strong>. A 90% chance
                          of precipitation persists, with freezing rain expected
                          before 10 AM, followed by freezing rain mixed with
                          ordinary rain as temperatures rise into the upper 30s.
                          Northeast winds of 2–8 mph remain relatively light.
                          Little or no ice accumulation is expected, although <strong>untreated
                            roads, bridges, and walkways may become slippery</strong>.
                          Cloudy skies and widespread precipitation continue
                          Friday night, with a 100% precipitation probability.
                          Rain before 7 PM transitions to rain showers through 1
                          AM, followed by rain and snow showers as temperatures
                          fall into the lower 30s. Northeast winds around 6 mph
                          accompany additional liquid precipitation amounts of
                          0.10–0.25 inch.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Saturday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Lingering rain and snow showers gradually diminish as
                          the low-pressure system moves away. Showers remain
                          likely before 8 AM, with a chance of <strong>additional
                            mixed precipitation </strong>through noon. A 60%
                          precipitation probability accompanies mostly cloudy
                          skies and temperatures reaching the upper 30s. North
                          winds of 5–8 mph reinforce the cooler conditions,
                          while additional liquid precipitation amounts remain
                          below 0.10 inch. Improving conditions develop during
                          the afternoon as precipitation becomes less
                          widespread. Cloudy skies continue Saturday night, with
                          temperatures falling into the upper 20s and west winds
                          of 1–7 mph. A 20% chance of snow showers develops
                          after 2 AM. Residual moisture may <strong>refreeze on
                            untreated surfaces </strong>where temperatures fall
                          below freezing.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Sunday, November 29 – Wednesday, December 2</strong></p>
                    <p><strong>Overview</strong></p>
                    <p><span>The <strong>unsettled Thanksgiving holiday pattern
                          continues</strong> as another low-pressure system
                        approaches the Saint John Valley, bringing renewed
                        opportunities for widespread rain and snow. Relatively
                        mild air ahead of the disturbance maintains temperatures
                        near or above freezing, complicating precipitation type
                        as atmospheric moisture increases late Sunday night. </span><span>A
                        100% precipitation probability Monday indicates <strong>widespread
                          rain and snow</strong>, although precipitation amounts
                        and the distribution of rain versus snow remain
                        uncertain.</span></p>
                    <p><span>Sheltered portions of the Saint John and Fish River
                        valleys may retain colder near-surface air, supporting
                        wet snow where precipitation persists or becomes
                        heavier. As the system departs Monday night, colder air
                        spreads across the region, increasing the potential for
                        <strong>wet or slushy surfaces to refreeze</strong>.
                        High pressure gradually restores drier, more seasonable
                        conditions through midweek, with nighttime cooling
                        becoming more pronounced. The primary uncertainties
                        involve precipitation type, accumulation amounts, and
                        potential travel impacts.</span></p>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Sunday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Mostly cloudy skies and relatively mild temperatures
                          provide a temporary break from the unsettled
                          Thanksgiving weather. Afternoon readings reach the
                          lower 40s, with southwest winds of 1–6 mph and
                          predominantly dry conditions. Mostly cloudy skies
                          continue Sunday night, with temperatures falling to
                          around 32°F. A slight chance of rain develops between
                          3 and 4 AM, followed by a 30% chance of rain and snow
                          as another low-pressure system approaches.
                          Precipitation type remains sensitive to small
                          temperature differences near freezing.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Monday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Widespread rain and snow develop as the approaching
                          disturbance spreads moisture across the region. Cloudy
                          skies and a 100% precipitation probability accompany
                          temperatures reaching the lower 40s. Rain becomes more
                          prevalent where temperatures rise above freezing,
                          although wet snow remains possible in colder locations
                          and during heavier precipitation. Snowfall and
                          rainfall amounts remain uncertain. Mostly cloudy skies
                          continue Monday night, with a 60% chance of
                          precipitation. Rain remains likely before 7 PM,
                          followed by a chance of rain and snow through 2 AM.
                          Temperatures fall into the upper 20s, creating the
                          potential for slippery untreated surfaces as residual
                          moisture freezes</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Tuesday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Partly sunny skies and colder temperatures prevail as
                          the departing disturbance allows drier air to spread
                          across the region. Afternoon readings reach the middle
                          30s, with predominantly dry conditions returning.
                          Partly cloudy skies continue Tuesday night, allowing
                          temperatures to fall into the lower 20s. Sheltered
                          portions of the Valley may experience locally colder
                          readings where winds diminish and nighttime cooling
                          becomes more efficient.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                      <li><span><span><strong>Wednesday:</strong></span></span><span>
                          Mostly sunny skies and seasonably cold conditions
                          continue as high pressure maintains predominantly dry
                          weather. Afternoon temperatures reach the middle 30s,
                          providing favorable conditions for outdoor activities.
                          Mostly cloudy skies develop Wednesday night, with
                          temperatures falling into the middle 20s. No
                          significant precipitation is indicated through the
                          overnight period.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Long Range Trends: Thursday, December 3 –
                        Wednesday, December 9</strong></p>
                    <p><span>Long-range trends favor<strong> above-normal
                          temperatures and precipitation</strong> across the
                        Saint John Valley into early December. A relatively mild
                        air mass and an active storm track across the Northeast
                        support periodic opportunities for rain, snow, or mixed
                        precipitation as disturbances move through the region.
                        Typical temperatures for this period feature daytime
                        highs in the upper 20s to lower 30s and overnight lows
                        in the mid-teens to lower 20s.</span></p>
                    <em><span style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note"><strong></strong></span></em>
                    <div><em><span style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note"><strong>Outlooks
                            </strong>updated 7-9 AM weekdays &amp; 8-10 AM
                          weekends and holidays.<strong> Long range outlooks</strong>
                          indicate broader temperature and precipitation trends
                          rather than precise day-to-day forecasts.&nbsp;<strong><br>
                            <br>
                            Common abbreviations</strong>:&nbsp;</span></em><em><span
                          style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note">CPC =
                          Climate Prediction Center;&nbsp;</span></em><em><span
                          style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note"> NHC =
                          National Hurricane Center;&nbsp;</span></em><em><span
                          style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note">NOAA =
                          National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration; NWS =
                          National Weather Service; </span></em><em><span style="font-size: 12px;"
                          class="small-note"></span></em><em><span style="font-size: 12px;"
                          class="small-note">SJV = Saint John Valley; S</span></em><em><span
                          style="font-size: 12px;" class="small-note">PC = Storm
                          Prediction Center;&nbsp;</span></em><em><span style="font-size: 12px;"
                          class="small-note">WPC = Weather Prediction Center</span></em></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div style="text-align: center;"> </div>
                <hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #eeeeee;
                  margin: 25px 0;">
                <!-- Daily Grid Overview -->
                <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 15px;">
                  <div style="text-align: center;"><strong style="font-size: 18px;">🌤️
                      7-Day Weather Summary for Fort Kent &amp; Vicinity <br>
                    </strong></div>
                  <div class="small-note"><span style="color: black;"><em>Updated
                        every 3-6 hours on the <a href="https://apexwx.github.io/"
                          moz-do-not-send="true">Apex Wx</a> website<br>
                        <br>
                      </em></span></div>
                </div>
                <b>Tonight: </b>A chance of snow after 5am. Mostly cloudy, with
                a low around 22. Calm wind. Chance of precipitation is 30%.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Friday: </b>Freezing rain before 10am, then rain or freezing
                rain between 10am and 11am, then rain after 11am. High near 38.
                Northeast wind 3 to 8 mph. Chance of precipitation is 90%. New
                ice accumulation of less than a 0.1 of an inch possible. <br>
                <br>
                <b>Friday Night: </b>Rain showers before 1am, then rain,
                possibly mixed with snow showers. Low around 33. Northeast wind
                around 6 mph. Chance of precipitation is 100%. Little or no snow
                accumulation expected. <br>
                <br>
                <b>Saturday: </b>Rain and snow showers likely before 9am, then
                a slight chance of rain showers between 9am and noon. Mostly
                cloudy, with a high near 39. Northwest wind 5 to 8 mph. Chance
                of precipitation is 60%. Little or no snow accumulation
                expected. <br>
                <br>
                <b>Saturday Night: </b>A slight chance of snow showers after
                2am. Cloudy, with a low around 28. Northwest wind around 6 mph
                becoming calm in the evening. Chance of precipitation is 20%.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Sunday: </b>Mostly cloudy, with a high near 41. Calm wind
                becoming southwest around 6 mph in the afternoon. <br>
                <br>
                <b>Sunday Night: </b>A slight chance of rain between 3am and
                4am, then a chance of rain and snow. Mostly cloudy, with a low
                around 32. Chance of precipitation is 30%.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Monday: </b>Rain and snow, becoming all rain after 10am.
                High near 42. Chance of precipitation is 100%.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Monday Night: </b>Rain and snow likely before 1am, then a
                slight chance of snow between 1am and 2am. Mostly cloudy, with a
                low around 28. Chance of precipitation is 60%.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Tuesday: </b>Mostly sunny, with a high near 34.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Tuesday Night: </b>Partly cloudy, with a low around 20.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Wednesday: </b>Mostly sunny, with a high near 35.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Wednesday Night:</b><span class="fw-day"></span>&nbsp; Mostly
                cloudy, with a low around 24.
                <div class="small-note" style="margin-top: 15px; text-align: left;"><em>Daily
                    Weather Summary data provided by <a href="https://www.weather.gov/car/"
                      moz-do-not-send="true">National Weather Service Forecast
                      Office</a> in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caribou,_Maine"
                      moz-do-not-send="true">Caribou, Maine</a></em> </div>
                <hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #eeeeee;
                  margin: 25px 0;">
                <!-- National Weather Outlook Summary Section -->
                <div style="text-align: center; font-size: 18px;"> <strong style="font-size: 18px;">🇺🇸
                    US National Weather Outlook</strong> </div>
                <div style="text-align: left; margin-top: 15px;
                  line-height: 22px;">
                  <div style="text-align: left;">
                    <div style="text-align: center;">
                      <div style="text-align: left;"><span style="
                                                      font-size:13px; font-weight:normal;                                    color:#555; display:block;                                                      margin-top:2px;"></span><strong></strong>
                        <dl>
                        </dl>
                        <span><strong>Heavy Rain, Localized Flooding, and Snow
                            Across Several Regions</strong></span>
                        <p><span>A low-pressure system moving northward from the
                            Southeast toward southeastern Canada brings heavy
                            rain to parts of New England Sunday into Monday.
                            Localized flash flooding threatens urban areas,
                            roads, small streams, and low-lying locations.
                            Scattered thunderstorms develop across the
                            Southeast, while additional showers reach southern
                            Florida Monday.</span></p>
                        <p><span>Another front spreads rain from the Upper
                            Midwest into the Mississippi Valley, while colder
                            air produces snow showers across Michigan's Upper
                            Peninsula and northern Lower Peninsula overnight
                            Sunday into Monday.</span></p>
                        <p><span>Along the western Gulf Coast, thunderstorms
                            bring heavy rainfall and localized flash-flooding
                            concerns through Monday morning. Meanwhile, high
                            pressure supports freezing temperatures across
                            portions of the Southwest, with freeze alerts
                            affecting New Mexico, Arizona, and western Texas.
                            Dense fog also develops along portions of the Gulf
                            Coast and Lower Mississippi Valley.</span></p>
                        <p><span>Across the West, a Pacific front produces rain
                            and mountain snow from the Northwest into northern
                            California and the Rockies. Snow reaches the Sierra
                            Nevada Sunday, followed by additional mountain snow
                            and lower-elevation rain across the central and
                            southern Rockies Monday.</span></p>
                        <div style="text-align: left;"><strong></strong></div>
                        <dl>
                        </dl>
                        <span></span> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                      <div><span></span><span></span><span> </span><span></span>
                      </div>
                    </div>
